window.INFO={"id":"sugar","title":"Sugar and South Sea Islanders in Queensland","subtitle":"An information bank about the sugar industry, the labour trade and the deportations of 1906 to 1908, with a focus on the Mackay district","description":"A non-fiction information bank about the Queensland sugar industry, South Sea Islander labour, the Pacific Island Labourers Act 1901 and the deportations of 1906 to 1908. Overview, timeline, places, people, real lives and sources.","dateLeft":"Queensland · 1863 to 1908","dateRight":"By Robin McKean","theme":"#1f2b1a","footnote":"This resource may contain names and images of deceased Aboriginal, Torres Strait Islander and South Sea Islander people. Everything in it comes from the sources listed on the Sources page.","footer":"Sugar and South Sea Islanders in Queensland. A non-fiction information bank by Robin McKean. This resource has no tracking and no cookies. An earlier choose-your-path story version is kept in the archive folder of the project on GitHub.","warning":["This resource may contain names and images of deceased Aboriginal, Torres Strait Islander and South Sea Islander people.","It is about the Queensland sugar industry, the labour trade that brought Pacific Islanders to Queensland (often called blackbirding), deaths from disease, the deportations of 1906 to 1908, and the dispossession of Aboriginal people. It does not show violence in detail, but it does say that it happened. Some old photographs show people who have died. You can stop at any time. If this upsets you, talk to a teacher, a family member or someone you trust."],"review":"Review status: this resource has not yet been reviewed by Australian South Sea Islander community groups or by Aboriginal Traditional Owners. The author is not a member of these communities. It should be reviewed before it is used widely, for example by the Queensland United Australian South Sea Islander Council (QUASSIC), local Australian South Sea Islander groups, and the Traditional Owners of the Mackay district, including the Yuibera people. Teachers, please seek advice from your local Australian South Sea Islander and Aboriginal communities before you use it in class.","sourcesIntro":"Every fact in this resource comes from one of these sources. Some sources are by historians, some by community organisations such as QUASSIC, and some by government bodies. Where sources describe the same events in different ways, the Sources page says so. The Folklore and legends page also uses some sources of this kind. They are listed here with the others, and the stories are labelled as unproven.","imageNote":"There are three old photographs in this resource. They are in the public domain in Australia or have no known copyright restrictions, and each is credited. They show people who have died. The people are not named in the captions. You can choose whether to look at them. There are no AI images.","overview":{"image":"mill","lead":"From 1863 about 50,000 Pacific Islanders were brought to Queensland on contracts to work, mostly in the sugar fields. Many were young men. Some were kidnapped, some were recruited by other means, and many joined freely. The labour trade ended in 1904. The Pacific Island Labourers Act 1901 led to deportations in 1906 to 1908.","glance":[{"n":"62,475","label":"indenture contracts for Pacific Islanders between 1863 and 1904. Many men signed more than once, so there were probably about 50,000 people","src":["quassic1"]},{"n":"More than 14,564","label":"Islanders died in Queensland between 1868 and 1906. That is about 24 per cent of the number of indenture contracts","src":["quassic6"]},{"n":"£6 a year","label":"the standard pay for a first-indenture labourer, which did not change for about forty years","src":["moore2015"]},{"n":"1,654","label":"the official number allowed to remain after the 1906 law. Research suggests about 2,500 did","src":["moore2000","naaSSI"]}],"sections":[{"h":"The labour trade","p":["The Queensland Government has records of 62,475 indenture contracts for Pacific Islanders between 1863 and 1904. About 95 per cent were adolescent or young adult males. Only about 5 per cent were women. There were 870 labour-recruiting voyages between Queensland and the Pacific Islands from 1863 to 1904. Recruiting for Queensland came mainly from the New Hebrides and the Solomon Islands. Around 80 islands in all were part of the trade.","QUASSIC says historians estimate that about 10 to 15 per cent of recruits were kidnapped as the law defined it. It says most kidnapping happened in the first ten or so years in any area, and that some continued until the 1890s. It also says that many young men joined freely, sometimes more than once. It adds that the whole process took people from their communities and bound them with legal contracts they did not understand.","The standard agreement was for three years, at £6 a year, with rations, clothing, basic accommodation, medical care and a return passage."],"src":["quassic1","quassic7","nma","slq","moore2015"]},{"h":"Words and their meanings","p":["The words “Kanaka” and “blackbirding” appear in the records and in books. “Kanaka” is now an offensive word, and this resource does not use it. “Blackbirding” is often used for the whole labour trade. QUASSIC explains that, in law, indenture was not the same as slavery: slavery was a lifelong legal status, and indenture was a contract for a set time. It also says that Australian South Sea Islanders call themselves the descendants of slaves, and that a deep sense of injustice cannot be answered by only denying that they were slaves."],"src":["quassic1"]},{"h":"Sugar and the Mackay district","p":["Pastoralists settled the Mackay district from 1862. John Spiller planted the first sugar cane in the district on 1 June 1865. About 70 Pacific Islanders arrived on the ship Prima Donna on 13 May 1867 to start work as indentured labourers on plantations around Mackay. By 1875, 23 sugar mills had been built in the district, before a cane disease damaged the crop. A second boom from 1879 to 1884 added 12 more mills.","The first government-supported central mills in the Mackay district opened at North Eton in 1888 and at Racecourse in 1889. After that, the plantation system declined faster. Sugar grew in other districts too, including Bundaberg, the Burdekin and Cairns."],"src":["pioneerMill","mackayTimeline","mackayCouncil","caneQld"]},{"h":"Work, pay and sickness","p":["The work in the cane fields included land clearing, holing, planting, weeding, trashing (stripping dead leaves), cutting, ratooning (growing a new crop from the old roots) and carting cane. Islanders worked barefoot in wet, hot fields. Between 1880 and 1900 a Pidgin English developed on the plantations and became a common language. Later it helped Islanders act together.","Government records show that more than 14,564 Islander labourers died in Queensland between 1868 and 1906. The real figure is probably closer to 15,000. The main killers were tuberculosis, pneumonia, bronchitis, pleurisy, dysentery, measles and chicken pox. The death rate in the first year of an indenture was about 81 per 1,000. The death rate for Europeans of all ages in Queensland was about 15 per 1,000.","The standard pay did not change for about forty years, while wages in the general workforce rose. Historian Clive Moore writes that it is a myth that Islanders were not paid, but the pay was far lower than the pay of European workers, and many Islanders received only part of their pay in cash. Not every person was paid. The activist Faith Bandler said that her father and his relatives were paid nothing."],"src":["slq","moore2000","quassic6","moore2015","nma"]},{"h":"Staying on, and the law","p":["Many people re-engaged without leaving Queensland. Moore writes that they became skilled in choosing employers and negotiating wages. By 1895 time-expired Islanders were 65 per cent of the Melanesians in the Mackay district. Many Islanders had bank accounts, possessions or property by the 1890s.","The Pacific Island Labourers Act 1901 was one of the first laws passed by the new Commonwealth Parliament. It was part of a package that made up the White Australia policy, with the Immigration Restriction Act 1901. Under it, no labour agreements were valid after the end of 1906, and after that date any person suspected of being a Pacific Islander had to prove that they were not. About 10,000 Pacific Islanders lived in Queensland and northern New South Wales when the Act passed."],"src":["moore2000","quassic6","naaPILA"]},{"h":"Deportation and exemption","p":["The Pacific Islanders’ Association, formed in Mackay in November 1901, and a petition of about 3,000 Islanders to King Edward VII in August 1902 protested against the law. A Queensland Royal Commission reported on 30 June 1906 and advised that more groups should be exempt from deportation. The final deportation began in late 1906 and continued until mid-1908.","The official number of Islanders allowed to remain was 1,654. Patricia Mercer’s research suggests that about 2,500 remained, so almost 1,000 avoided deportation. Today the descendants of Pacific Islanders brought to Australia are a distinct cultural group, called the Australian South Sea Islander community. In 2000 the Queensland Government recognised South Sea Islanders as a distinct cultural group and acknowledged the discrimination and injustice they had experienced."],"src":["moore2000","naaPILA","naaSSI","nma"]},{"h":"Aboriginal people in the sugar districts","p":["Each sugar district is on Aboriginal country. Aboriginal people resisted the settlers who took their land. Widespread frontier violence happened across the Mackay district in the 1860s and 1870s. The Queensland Government history says that the Native Police were important in crushing Aboriginal resistance. In 1897 Queensland passed a law under which local Protectors controlled Aboriginal labour and savings, and could remove people to reserves and missions. From 1898 to 1967 there were 72 documented removals of Aboriginal people from the Mackay district."],"src":["sarina","senate","yarrabah","bundabergCouncil"]}],"words":[["Indenture","A contract for a set time. In this history it was usually for three years."],["Pacific Islander, Islander","The main terms used in this resource. “Australian South Sea Islander” is the term used today for the descendants. The community may have other preferred terms."],["Blackbirding","A word often used for the whole labour trade. Historians separate kidnapping from other kinds of recruiting."],["Government Agent","An inspector required on recruiting ships from 1880."],["Time-expired labourer","A labourer who had finished one agreement."],["Ticket-holder","A labourer who had arrived before 1 September 1879 and was bound only by the ordinary laws of Queensland."],["Central mill","A large mill supplied by many farms, paid for by loans from the government."],["Protector","A government official who controlled Aboriginal labour and savings under the Queensland laws from 1897."]],"left":["This resource is mainly about the Mackay district, with short notes on Bundaberg, the Burdekin and Cairns. It does not cover the Torres Strait, other industries where Islanders worked, the Chinese, Japanese, Javanese and other workers in the sugar districts, or the lives of Islander women and children in detail.","It does not describe the islands in detail, because the people there were many different peoples, and the author has no right to speak for them.","It ends in 1907 and 1908 and does not cover what happened later to the people who stayed, apart from short notes.","It does not describe violence on recruiting ships or against Aboriginal people in detail. It mentions it briefly because it is part of the history.","It does not use the word “Kanaka”, which is offensive today."]},"timeline":[{"date":"1863","title":"The labour trade begins","text":"The Queensland Government has records of 62,475 indenture contracts for Pacific Islanders between 1863 and 1904. Many men signed more than once, so there were probably about 50,000 individual people. There were 870 labour-recruiting voyages from 1863 to 1904.","src":["quassic1","quassic7"]},{"date":"1 June 1865","title":"The first sugar cane in the Mackay district","text":"John Spiller planted the first sugar cane in the district. Pastoralists had settled the district from 1862.","src":["pioneerMill","mackayTimeline"]},{"date":"1866 and 1868","title":"Native Police barracks","text":"Native Police barracks were built at Marlborough in 1866 and at Bloomsbury, just north of Mackay, in 1868.","src":["sarina"]},{"date":"13 May 1867","title":"Pacific Islanders arrive at Mackay","text":"About 70 Pacific Islanders arrived on the ship Prima Donna to start work as indentured labourers on plantations around Mackay.","src":["pioneerMill","mackayTimeline"]},{"date":"1868","title":"Polynesian Labourers Act; first mill at Mackay","text":"The Polynesian Labourers Act 1868 was the first Queensland Act made for the labour trade. It required employers to apply for approval, banned recruiters from kidnapping, set minimum standards for the voyage and set agreements of three years. The Alexandra mill began crushing on 15 September 1868.","src":["uq","moore2015","alexandra"]},{"date":"1871","title":"A Government Agent is chained; an Aboriginal reserve is set aside","text":"In 1871 the Government Agent on the Jason tried to stop abuses. The captain had him handcuffed and chained to a ring-bolt for more than three weeks. In the same year the government set aside 14,080 acres near Cape Palmerstone and Homebush as an Aboriginal reserve. The area was reduced to 10,000 acres by 1878. In 1879 funding was cut, the school closed and 90 per cent of the reserve was opened for settlement.","src":["quassic7","sarina"]},{"date":"1875","title":"Twenty-three mills in the Mackay district","text":"By 1875, 23 sugar mills had been built in the district, before a cane disease damaged the crop. A second boom from 1879 to 1884 added 12 more mills.","src":["mackayCouncil"]},{"date":"1880","title":"Pacific Island Labourers Act","text":"The Act replaced the 1868 Act. It made forced recruiting illegal and required inspectors, called Government Agents, on the ships. It also put a bond on ship owners so that they delivered passengers and their property back to the correct island at the end of their agreements. The National Museum of Australia says that agents were not always careful and some could be bribed by the crew.","src":["nma","moore2015"]},{"date":"1883 and 1884","title":"Mills and plantations in other districts","text":"The Hambledon mill near Cairns began crushing in 1883. The Pioneer partnership in the Burdekin began in 1883 and had five plantations and a mill by 1884. At Bundaberg the South Sea Islander Wall at Mon Repos was built about 1884, probably by Islander labour, to clear stony land for cane. It is about 1,580 metres long and is listed on the Queensland Heritage Register.","src":["hambledon","jcu","wall"]},{"date":"1884","title":"The Hopeful voyages and the Amendment Act","text":"The voyages of the Hopeful went to islands east of New Guinea. The crew were tried in Brisbane in late 1884. A Royal Commission said the cruise was “one long record of deceit, cruel treachery, deliberate kidnapping and cold-blooded murder”. The men were found guilty. The death sentences were later commuted (reduced). The 1884 Amendment Act limited newly arrived labourers to field work in tropical agriculture. The death rate for Islanders in Queensland reached its highest point in 1884, at 147 per 1,000.","src":["quassic7","docdem","quassic6"]},{"date":"6 May 1885","title":"A newspaper report on the Hopeful","text":"A report describes men pulled on board and told that they were to go and work in a white man’s country “for one or two moons”.","src":["trove1885"]},{"date":"1885","title":"Pacific Islanders’ Fund; a law meant to end recruiting","text":"The Queensland Government set up the Pacific Islanders’ Fund. It held the unpaid wages of Islanders who died. Clive Moore found that the government seldom returned the full wages to the families of the dead, and used the money for other costs of the labour trade. In 1885 Queensland also passed a law meant to end recruiting. In 1892 Parliament repealed it and the labour trade continued until 1904.","flag":"Sources describe the 1885 to 1892 changes in different ways. The National Museum of Australia says that a ban in 1891 was postponed. This resource follows the State Library of Queensland blog.","src":["moore2015","slq","nma"]},{"date":"1888 and 1889","title":"Central mills open","text":"The first government-supported central mills in the Mackay district opened at North Eton in 1888 and at Racecourse in 1889. In 1888 the Hambledon mill employed 176 South Sea Islanders, 29 Chinese, 6 Javanese and 32 Europeans.","src":["mackayCouncil","caneQld","hambledon"]},{"date":"1892","title":"Yarrabah mission founded","text":"Aboriginal people and some South Sea Islanders were relocated by government to Yarrabah, in the Cairns district.","src":["yarrabah"]},{"date":"1893","title":"Sugar Works Guarantee Act","text":"The Act allowed the Queensland Government to lend money for central mills. Farmers formed companies and mortgaged their land to secure the loans.","src":["caneQld","mackayCouncil"]},{"date":"1895","title":"Time-expired Islanders in Mackay","text":"Time-expired Islanders were 65 per cent of the Melanesians in the Mackay district.","src":["moore2000","quassic6"]},{"date":"1896","title":"A regulation on time-expired Islanders","text":"Queensland made a regulation that every time-expired Islander must, within one month after an agreement ended, either return to the islands or sign a new agreement with an employer.","src":["moore2000"]},{"date":"1897 to 1904","title":"Laws controlling Aboriginal workers","text":"In 1897 Queensland passed the Aboriginals Protection and Restriction of the Sale of Opium Act. Under it and later Acts, local Protectors controlled Aboriginal labour and savings, and could remove people to reserves and missions. An Aboriginal worker could only be employed under a permit from a Protector, and an employer had to make an agreement of no more than 12 months. In 1901 a minimum wage of five shillings was set for most workers, which the Senate committee says was less than one-eighth of the white wage. From 1904 wages could be paid directly to the Protector.","src":["senate","sarina"]},{"date":"November 1901","title":"The Pacific Islanders’ Association is formed; Premier Philp writes","text":"The Pacific Islanders’ Association was formed in Mackay. Queensland’s Premier, Robert Philp, wrote that deporting people who had come as legal immigrants would cause “great inhumanity and injustice”.","src":["moore2000"]},{"date":"17 December 1901","title":"The Pacific Island Labourers Act receives assent","text":"The Act was one of the first laws passed by the new Commonwealth Parliament. It was part of a package that made up the White Australia policy, with the Immigration Restriction Act 1901. Labourers could enter under licence until 31 March 1904, in limited numbers. About 10,000 Pacific Islanders lived in Queensland and northern New South Wales when the Act passed. About 700 were ticket-holders who were exempt.","src":["naaPILA","moore2000"]},{"date":"August 1902","title":"A petition to King Edward VII","text":"The Colonial Office received a petition from about 3,000 Islanders. About 1,800 of the signatures were collected by Fanny Nicol, a missionary at Bundaberg. The King was advised to refuse it, but it embarrassed the Australian Government.","src":["moore2000"]},{"date":"1902 and 1903","title":"Limits on new arrivals","text":"In 1902 entry was limited to three-quarters of the number who had left in 1901. In 1903 it was limited to half of the 1902 departures. In 1901, 839 Islanders returned to the islands and another 1,678 arrived. At the end of 1904, after recruiting ended, there were still 7,879 Islanders in Queensland.","src":["naaPILA","moore2000"]},{"date":"30 June 1906","title":"The Queensland Royal Commission reports","text":"It advised that more groups should be exempt from deportation. It found that some 4,000 to 4,500 Islanders were liable for deportation.","src":["moore2000"]},{"date":"7 August to 12 October 1906","title":"Deakin accepts most recommendations; amendment receives assent","text":"On 7 August 1906 Prime Minister Deakin accepted most of the recommendations, and the amendment received assent on 12 October 1906. The new exemption groups were Islanders who had lived continuously in Australia since 31 December 1886, the aged and infirm, those with children educated in state schools, owners of freehold land, those married before October 1906 to a partner not from their own island, and those who could show that their lives would be in danger if returned home.","flag":"Sources give slightly different dates for the marriage cut-off in October 1906, so it is given only as “October 1906”.","src":["moore2000","mcneill"]},{"date":"13 September to 1 October 1906","title":"Henry Tongoa and Alick Mallicoola go to Melbourne","text":"The two men arrived in Melbourne on 13 September 1906. They met Prime Minister Deakin on 17 September and 1 October and gave him a petition from 427 members of the Association. Deakin said that the deportation provision would not be reversed.","src":["moore2000"]},{"date":"End of 1906","title":"No labour agreements valid","text":"No labour agreements were valid after the end of 1906. After that date any person suspected of being a Pacific Islander had to prove that they were not. The final deportation began in late 1906.","src":["naaPILA"]},{"date":"4 May 1907","title":"A General Certificate of Exemption","text":"One General Certificate of Exemption, issued to an Islander known as Calleyho, is dated 4 May 1907.","src":["peo"]},{"date":"31 July 1908","title":"The Pacific Island Branch closes","text":"The deportation continued until mid-1908. It took longer than the government had planned. The Pacific Island Branch of the Queensland Immigration Department closed on 31 July 1908.","src":["naaPILA","moore2000"]},{"date":"2000","title":"Recognition by the Queensland Government","text":"The Queensland Government recognised South Sea Islanders as a distinct cultural group and acknowledged the discrimination and injustice they had experienced.","src":["naaSSI","nma"]}],"places":[{"name":"Mackay","where":"Central Queensland coast","image":"mill","text":["The first sugar cane in the district was planted in 1865, and about 70 Pacific Islanders arrived on the Prima Donna in 1867. The photograph above shows the Pioneer Sugar Mill at Mackay in the 1880s. By 1895 time-expired Islanders were 65 per cent of the Melanesians in the Mackay district. The Pacific Islanders’ Association was formed in Mackay in November 1901.","The Queensland Government history names the Yuibera, Wiri, Barada Barna Kabalbara and Yetimarla, Birri-Gubba and Kungalburra peoples for the nearby Sarina area. From 1898 to 1967 there were 72 documented removals of Aboriginal people from the Mackay district, most to Yarrabah, Barambah (now Cherbourg), Woorabinda and Palm Island."],"src":["pioneerMill","mackayTimeline","moore2000","sarina"]},{"name":"Marlborough, Bloomsbury and Cape Palmerstone","where":"Mackay and Sarina districts","text":["Native Police barracks were built at Marlborough in 1866 and at Bloomsbury in 1868. In 1871 the government set aside 14,080 acres near Cape Palmerstone and Homebush as an Aboriginal reserve. The area was reduced to 10,000 acres by 1878. In 1879 funding was cut, the school closed and 90 per cent of the reserve was opened for settlement. The residents had to move away. Some Aboriginal men had been hired out to local employers as labourers while the reserve operated."],"src":["sarina"]},{"name":"Bundaberg","where":"Taribelang Bunda, Gooreng Gooreng, Gurang and Bailai Country","image":"bundaberg","text":["The South Sea Islander Wall at Mon Repos was built about 1884, probably by Islander labour, to clear stony land for cane. It is about 1,580 metres long and is listed on the Queensland Heritage Register. Fanny Nicol, a missionary at Bundaberg, collected about 1,800 signatures for the 1902 petition to King Edward VII.","Bundaberg Regional Council acknowledges the Taribelang Bunda, Gooreng Gooreng, Gurang and Bailai Peoples as Traditional Owners. The photograph above shows South Sea Islanders on the deck of a ship arriving at Bundaberg in 1895."],"src":["wall","moore2000","bundabergCouncil","photoBundaberg"]},{"name":"The Burdekin","where":"North Queensland","text":["The Pioneer partnership began in 1883 and had five plantations and a mill by 1884. Low rainfall was a problem, and the partners started irrigation schemes."],"src":["jcu"]},{"name":"Hambledon and Cairns","where":"Far North Queensland","image":"hambledon","text":["The Hambledon mill near Cairns began crushing in 1883. In 1888 it employed 176 South Sea Islanders, 29 Chinese, 6 Javanese and 32 Europeans. The photograph above shows South Sea Islander workers hoeing weeds on a sugar cane plantation at Hambledon about 1891.","The Queensland Government history of the Cairns area records that Native Police “dispersals” killed many Yidindji and Djabugay people, and that Yarrabah mission was founded in 1892, where Aboriginal people and some South Sea Islanders were relocated by government."],"src":["hambledon","photoHambledon","yarrabah"]},{"name":"The islands of the labour trade","where":"The New Hebrides, the Solomon Islands and around 80 islands in all","text":["Recruiting for Queensland came mainly from the New Hebrides and the Solomon Islands. Around 80 islands in all were part of the trade, including islands that are now part of Vanuatu, Solomon Islands, New Caledonia, Fiji, Kiribati and Papua New Guinea. The hundreds of languages of Melanesia made it hard for islanders from different places to talk. The 1902 petition argued that some home islands had become German possessions and that tribal lands had been sold, so families might not welcome people back."],"src":["nma","moore2000"]}],"people":[{"kind":"group","name":"Pacific Islander labourers","text":"About 50,000 people on 62,475 contracts. About 95 per cent were adolescent or young adult males. Only about 5 per cent were women. Most came from the New Hebrides and the Solomon Islands.","src":["quassic1","nma"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Time-expired labourers and ticket-holders","text":"Time-expired labourers had finished one agreement. Ticket-holders had arrived before 1 September 1879 and were bound only by the ordinary laws of Queensland. About 700 ticket-holders were exempt from the 1901 Act.","src":["docdem","naaPILA","moore2000"]},{"kind":"group","name":"The Pacific Islanders’ Association","dates":"Formed in Mackay, November 1901","text":"In 1906 it met regularly in Mackay, hired a solicitor, set by-laws and formed branches at Proserpine and Bowen. The Association was not united: Rockhampton Islanders saw themselves as separate from the Mackay group, and New Hebrideans and Solomon Islanders often did not work together. Moore says that the Islanders’ leaders were able to work in both Melanesian and Australian society.","src":["moore2000"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Australian South Sea Islander community","text":"The descendants of the Pacific Islanders brought to Australia. They are a distinct cultural group. Many have shared South Sea Islander and Aboriginal heritage.","src":["naaSSI","nma"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Aboriginal peoples of the sugar districts","text":"Each sugar district is on Aboriginal country. The Queensland Government history names the Yuibera, Wiri, Barada Barna Kabalbara and Yetimarla, Birri-Gubba and Kungalburra peoples for the Sarina area; Bundaberg Regional Council names the Taribelang Bunda, Gooreng Gooreng, Gurang and Bailai Peoples; and the Queensland Government history of the Cairns area names the Yidindji and Djabugay peoples.","src":["sarina","bundabergCouncil","yarrabah"]},{"kind":"person","name":"John Spiller","dates":"Mackay, 1865","text":"Planted the first sugar cane in the Mackay district on 1 June 1865.","src":["pioneerMill"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Henry Tongoa and Alick Mallicoola","dates":"Melbourne, 1906","text":"Leaders of the Pacific Islanders’ Association who travelled to Melbourne and met Prime Minister Deakin. See Real lives.","src":["moore2000"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Fanny Nicol","dates":"Bundaberg, 1902","text":"A missionary at Bundaberg who collected about 1,800 signatures for the 1902 petition.","src":["moore2000"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Robert Philp","dates":"Premier of Queensland, 1901","text":"Wrote in November 1901 that deporting people who had come as legal immigrants would cause “great inhumanity and injustice”.","src":["moore2000"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Alfred Deakin","dates":"Prime Minister, 1906","text":"Accepted most of the Queensland Royal Commission’s recommendations on 7 August 1906. He met Henry Tongoa and Alick Mallicoola on 17 September and 1 October 1906.","src":["moore2000"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Faith Bandler","text":"An activist who said that her father and his relatives were paid nothing. She said she thought of it as a form of slavery.","src":["nma"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Calleyho","dates":"Certificate dated 4 May 1907","text":"An Islander named on a General Certificate of Exemption, one of the documents issued under the 1901 Act and its 1906 amendment.","src":["peo"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Clive Moore and Patricia Mercer","dates":"Historians","text":"Moore’s research is the main source here for the Islander community and the deportations. Mercer’s research suggests that about 2,500 Islanders remained after 1906, not the official 1,654.","src":["moore2000","moore2015","naaSSI"]},{"kind":"org","name":"QUASSIC","text":"The Queensland United Australian South Sea Islander Council. It publishes historical information sheets on the labour trade.","src":["quassic1","quassic6","quassic7"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Government Agents","text":"Inspectors required on recruiting ships from 1880. The National Museum of Australia says agents were not always careful and some could be bribed by the crew.","src":["nma","moore2015"]},{"kind":"org","name":"The Queensland Royal Commission of 1906","text":"Reported on 30 June 1906 and advised that more groups should be exempt from deportation.","src":["moore2000"]},{"kind":"org","name":"Protectors","text":"Government officials who controlled Aboriginal labour and savings under the Queensland laws from 1897.","src":["senate","sarina"]}],"lives":[{"name":"Henry Tongoa and Alick Mallicoola","dates":"Melbourne, September and October 1906","text":["In 1906 the Pacific Islanders’ Association met regularly in Mackay, hired a solicitor, set by-laws and formed branches at Proserpine and Bowen. Henry Tongoa and Alick Mallicoola travelled to Melbourne and arrived on 13 September 1906. They met Prime Minister Deakin on 17 September and 1 October and gave him a petition from 427 members. Deakin said that the deportation provision would not be reversed."],"src":["moore2000"]},{"name":"Fanny Nicol and the petition of 1902","dates":"Bundaberg, 1902","text":["In August 1902 the Colonial Office received a petition to King Edward VII from about 3,000 Islanders. About 1,800 of the signatures were collected by Fanny Nicol, a missionary at Bundaberg. The King was advised to refuse it, but it embarrassed the Australian Government. The petition argued that some home islands had become German possessions and that tribal lands had been sold, so families might not welcome people back."],"src":["moore2000"]},{"name":"Robert Philp, Premier of Queensland","dates":"November 1901","text":["Robert Philp wrote in November 1901 about the new law. His words, as quoted by Moore, are below."],"quote":{"lines":["great inhumanity and injustice"],"by":"Robert Philp, November 1901, as quoted by Clive Moore. He wrote that deporting people who had come as legal immigrants would cause these."},"src":["moore2000"]},{"name":"Faith Bandler’s father and his relatives","dates":"Recalled by Faith Bandler","text":["The activist Faith Bandler said that her father and his relatives were paid nothing. She said she thought of it as a form of slavery. Not every Islander was paid nothing: Clive Moore writes that it is a myth that Islanders were not paid, but the standard pay was far lower than the pay of European workers and many received only part of it in cash."],"flag":"These two accounts are different. The first is one person’s recollection of her family. The second is a historian’s general finding. Both are given.","src":["nma","moore2015"]},{"name":"Calleyho","dates":"Certificate dated 4 May 1907","text":["A General Certificate of Exemption, issued to an Islander known as Calleyho, is dated 4 May 1907. It is one of the certificates issued under the Pacific Island Labourers Act 1901 and its 1906 amendment, which allowed some people to stay. The official number allowed to remain was 1,654."],"src":["peo","moore2000","naaSSI"]},{"name":"The men of the Hopeful, 1884","dates":"Voyages of 1884","text":["The Hopeful made voyages in 1884 to islands east of New Guinea. The crew were tried in Brisbane in late 1884. A Royal Commission said the cruise was “one long record of deceit, cruel treachery, deliberate kidnapping and cold-blooded murder”. A newspaper report of 6 May 1885 describes men pulled on board and told that they were to go and work in a white man’s country “for one or two moons”. The names of the men who were taken are not given in the sources read here."],"quote":{"lines":["one long record of deceit, cruel treachery, deliberate kidnapping and cold-blooded murder"],"by":"Royal Commission on the Hopeful voyages, as quoted by QUASSIC."},"src":["quassic7","trove1885"]}],"know":[{"t":"Only about 5 per cent of indentured Pacific Islanders were women, so most plantation workforces were almost all male.","src":["quassic1"]},{"t":"The death rate in the first year of an indenture was about 81 per 1,000. The death rate for Europeans of all ages in Queensland was about 15 per 1,000.","src":["quassic6"]},{"t":"Between 1880 and 1900 a Pidgin English developed on the plantations and became a common language for Islanders from different places. Later it helped Islanders act together.","src":["moore2000"]},{"t":"The standard pay of £6 a year did not change for about forty years, while wages in the general workforce rose.","src":["moore2015"]},{"t":"The South Sea Islander Wall at Mon Repos near Bundaberg is about 1,580 metres long and is listed on the Queensland Heritage Register.","src":["wall"]},{"t":"By 1895 time-expired Islanders were 65 per cent of the Melanesians in the Mackay district.","src":["moore2000","quassic6"]},{"t":"By 1905, 78 per cent of farmers and labour in the sugar industry were white, and by 1911 it was 94 per cent. The Commonwealth paid a bounty to farmers and millers who used only white labour.","src":["moore2000","mcneill"]},{"t":"McNeill and Marmo write that 17 of the people deported would otherwise have met the criteria to stay, but had married Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander people.","src":["mcneill"]},{"t":"Some people deported in 1906 to 1908 had arrived in Australia at such a young age that they had no memory of their home country.","src":["nma"]},{"t":"An estimated 900 people hid in the bush to avoid deportation. This estimate is from a 2019 magazine article cited in a research paper.","src":["mcneill"]},{"t":"In 2000 the Queensland Government recognised South Sea Islanders as a distinct cultural group.","src":["naaSSI","nma"]}],"disagree":["Sources give slightly different dates for the marriage cut-off in October 1906. This resource gives it only as “October 1906”.","Sources describe the 1885 to 1892 changes in different ways. The National Museum of Australia says that a ban in 1891 was postponed. This resource follows the State Library of Queensland blog: a law of 1885 meant to end recruiting was repealed in 1892.","The official number of Islanders allowed to remain was 1,654, but Patricia Mercer’s research suggests about 2,500 remained. The figure of 900 hiding is an estimate from a 2019 magazine article, cited in a research paper, and is second-hand.","QUASSIC says historians estimate that about 10 to 15 per cent of recruits were kidnapped as the law defined it. This is an estimate of kidnapping as the law defined it.","Historians separate kidnapping from other kinds of recruiting. Whether the system should be called slavery is not decided here. QUASSIC explains that indenture was not legally slavery, and also that Australian South Sea Islanders call themselves descendants of slaves. Both are given.","The 1884 voyages of the Hopeful are described from QUASSIC’s summary of a Royal Commission’s words and from a newspaper report. The Royal Commission report itself was not read.","The names of the Aboriginal peoples of the Mackay area are not given the same way in all sources. The Queensland Government history of the Sarina area names several peoples. The review note names the Yuibera people. The spellings should be checked with the peoples concerned.","The Folklore and legends page contains stories that are not proven. Each is labelled “Folklore: unproven” and says what is documented and what is doubtful. These stories are not used as facts anywhere else in this resource."],"care":["This resource may contain names and images of deceased Aboriginal, Torres Strait Islander and South Sea Islander people. It says that violence and deaths from disease happened, and it does not describe violence in detail.","People in this history made choices. In real history, Pacific Islanders chose, argued, petitioned, organised, saved, bought land, raised families and kept their communities together. Aboriginal people are people with their own business and not only helpers or victims.","The word “Kanaka” is in the records and is offensive today. This resource does not use it. Some source documents use words and attitudes that are offensive today. They are quoted very rarely, and only where needed.","This resource was written by an author who is not a member of these communities. It has not yet been reviewed by Australian South Sea Islander community groups, such as QUASSIC, or by Aboriginal Traditional Owners, such as the Yuibera people for Mackay. Please seek that review before using it widely, and tell the author if anything needs to change."],"readMore":["qsa"],"images":{"mill":{"file":"images/pioneer-mill-mackay-1885.jpg","w":1000,"h":774,"people":true,"summary":"the Pioneer Sugar Mill at Mackay in the 1880s (it shows people who have died)","alt":"A black and white photograph of the Pioneer Sugar Mill at Mackay, with wooden mill buildings, two brick chimneys, a large heap of cut cane in front and a group of workers standing near the buildings.","caption":"Pioneer Sugar Mill at Mackay, about 1885. The people in the photograph are not named.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_13930_Pioneer_Sugar_Mill_at_Mackay,_1880s.jpg"},"bundaberg":{"file":"images/bundaberg-1895.jpg","w":1000,"h":822,"people":true,"summary":"South Sea Islanders on a ship at Bundaberg, 1895 (it shows people who have died)","alt":"A black and white photograph of many South Sea Islanders standing close together on the deck of a sailing ship at Bundaberg in 1895. Ropes and masts cross the picture, and one white man stands at the right edge.","caption":"South Sea Islanders on the deck of a ship arriving at Bundaberg, 1895. Photograph by R. J. Cottell, from Pugh’s Almanac 1895.","credit":"State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_50564_South_Sea_Islanders_on_the_deck_of_a_ship_arriving_at_Bundaberg,_1895.jpg"},"hambledon":{"file":"images/hambledon-1891.jpg","w":1000,"h":749,"people":true,"summary":"workers on a plantation at Hambledon, about 1891 (it shows people who have died)","alt":"A black and white photograph of about a dozen men standing among rows of young sugar cane at Hambledon near Cairns, about 1891. Some hold hoes. Hills are behind them and the words “Hoeing weeds in young cane” are printed on the photograph.","caption":"South Sea Islander workers hoeing weeds on a sugar cane plantation at Hambledon, Queensland, about 1891.","credit":"State Library of Queensland (photographer unidentified; no known copyright restrictions)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australian_South_Sea_Islander_workers_hoeing_weeds_on_a_sugar_cane_plantation_in_Hambledon,_Queensland,_ca._1891_(9205456322).jpg"}},"teacherNotes":["Before you use this in class, please seek advice from Australian South Sea Islander community groups in your area (for example QUASSIC) and from local Aboriginal Traditional Owners. The resource was written by an author who is not part of these communities, and it has not yet been reviewed by them.","Give students the content warning before they start. Tell them they may stop at any time. Check whether any students have family connections to this history. Many Australian South Sea Islander and Aboriginal families do. Offer an alternative task if needed.","A suggested lesson: 10 minutes to read the content warning and discuss respect; 25 minutes reading the Overview, Timeline and Real lives; 20 minutes for discussion and one writing task.","The answers are suggestions. They are behind “Show” buttons. The student version has no answers in its code.","The resource does not describe violence in detail. Students may ask what happened. Use the sources to answer plainly and calmly.","The harder questions are about fairness: the way marriage to an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person was treated in the deportations, and what choices were really free. There are no simple answers. Ask students to use evidence."],"discuss":[{"q":"QUASSIC says historians estimate that about 10 to 15 per cent of recruits were kidnapped, and that many young men joined freely. Why do the sources also say the process “bound them with legal contracts they did not understand”? What does this tell us about the word “free”?","a":["The sources say the whole process took people from their communities and bound them with legal contracts they did not understand. So a person could join without being kidnapped and still not have a free choice in the full sense.","There is no single right answer. Students should use the evidence and say what they cannot tell from it."]},{"q":"In law, indenture was not the same as slavery, but Australian South Sea Islanders call themselves the descendants of slaves. How does QUASSIC explain this? Why might both statements be true?","a":["Slavery was a lifelong legal status. Indenture was a contract for a set time. QUASSIC also says that a deep sense of injustice cannot be answered by only denying that they were slaves.","Students may say that a legal definition and a community’s experience can be different, and that both are evidence."]},{"q":"More than 14,564 Islanders died in Queensland between 1868 and 1906. What does QUASSIC say were the main causes? Who does it say can be blamed?","a":["Tuberculosis, pneumonia, bronchitis, pleurisy, dysentery, measles and chicken pox. QUASSIC says exposure to new diseases was the main cause of death, and that employers and the government can be blamed for keeping the system going for forty years after the death rate was known."]},{"q":"The Pacific Islanders’ Fund held the unpaid wages of Islanders who died. What did Clive Moore find? Why does it matter who kept the records?","a":["He found that the government seldom returned the full wages to the families of the dead, and used the money for other costs of the labour trade.","Students might say that the people who kept the money also kept the records, and that families could not easily check them."]},{"q":"Read the exemption groups in the Timeline (30 June to 12 October 1906). Which would you expect to protect the most people? What did McNeill and Marmo find about people who had married Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander people?","a":["The groups were: lived in Australia since 31 December 1886; the aged and infirm; those with children in state schools; owners of freehold land; those married before October 1906 to a partner not from their own island; those whose lives would be in danger if returned. Students should give a reasoned answer.","McNeill and Marmo write that 17 of the people deported would otherwise have met the criteria to stay, but had married Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander people."]},{"q":"The official number allowed to remain was 1,654, and Mercer’s research suggests about 2,500 did. An estimated 900 people hid. How reliable are these numbers? What does it mean to say a number is “second-hand”?","a":["The official number is from government records. Mercer’s figure is from research, and the figure of 900 is from a magazine article cited in a research paper, so it is second-hand. Students should say that the estimates should be checked before they are repeated."]},{"q":"Henry Tongoa, Alick Mallicoola, Fanny Nicol and the Pacific Islanders’ Association acted against the law. What did they do? What does this show about how Islanders took part in politics?","a":["They formed an association, hired a solicitor, petitioned the King (about 3,000 signatures) and travelled to Melbourne to meet the Prime Minister with a petition from 427 members. Deakin said the deportation provision would not be reversed.","Students may note that Islander leaders worked in both Melanesian and Australian society, and that the Association was not united."]},{"q":"Why do the notes say that people in this history “chose, argued, petitioned, organised, saved, bought land, raised families and kept their communities together”? Find two facts in this resource that support this.","a":["Examples: many re-engaged and became skilled in choosing employers and negotiating wages; many had bank accounts, possessions or property by the 1890s; the Association hired a solicitor; the 1902 petition."]},{"q":"What would you want to ask Australian South Sea Islander community groups before using this resource with a class? List three questions.","a":["Examples: Which parts are wrong, or hurt? Which words do you prefer? Is there a story or a name that should be told by community members and not by the author? Is it all right to show these photographs?"]}],"sources":{"alexandra":{"t":"Mackay history research. Alexandra Sugar Mill, 1868–1884.","u":"http://www.mackayhistory.org/research/sugar_mills/alexandra.html","short":"Alexandra Sugar Mill, Mackay history"},"bundabergCouncil":{"t":"Bundaberg Regional Council. Acknowledgement of Country (Taribelang Bunda, Gooreng Gooreng, Gurang and Bailai Peoples).","u":"https://www.ourbundabergregion.com.au/acknowledgement-of-country","short":"Bundaberg Regional Council"},"hambledon":{"t":"Canetrains wiki. Hambledon Mill (Edmonton, near Cairns). Cites the Australian Dictionary of Biography entry for Thomas Swallow.","u":"https://canetrains.net/wiki/Hambledon_Mill","short":"Canetrains, Hambledon Mill"},"caneQld":{"t":"Canetrains wiki. Queensland (history of the sugar industry, including the Sugar Works Guarantee Act 1893).","u":"https://canetrains.net/wiki/Queensland","short":"Canetrains, Queensland"},"docdem":{"t":"Museum of Australian Democracy, Documenting Democracy. Pacific Island Labourers Act Amendment Act 1884 (Qld). Original held by Queensland State Archives, SRS 1296/1/17.","u":"https://www.foundingdocs.gov.au/item-sdid-53.html","short":"Documenting Democracy, 1884 Act"},"jcu":{"t":"James Cook University Library Archives. Pioneer Sugar Mill Archive (Burdekin district).","u":"https://libserver.jcu.edu.au/specials/Archives/pioneer.html","short":"JCU Library, Pioneer Sugar Mill Archive"},"mackayTimeline":{"t":"Mackay Family History and Historical Society. Mackay Timeline: a chronological history of Mackay events.","u":"http://mackayhistory.org/research/timeline/timeline.html","short":"Mackay history timeline"},"mackayCouncil":{"t":"Mackay Regional Council (2008). Mackay history lift-out: “A new port in Queensland” and “Sugar boom”.","u":"https://www.mackay.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/19490/library_12_page_lift-out.pdf","short":"Mackay Regional Council history lift-out"},"mcneill":{"t":"McNeill, H. and Marmo, M. (2023). Past–present differential inclusion: Australia’s targeted deportation of Pacific Islanders, 1901 to 2021. International Journal for Crime, Justice and Social Democracy, 12(1), 42–56.","u":"https://doi.org/10.5204/ijcjsd.2743","short":"McNeill and Marmo (2023)"},"moore2000":{"t":"Moore, C. (2000). “Good-bye, Queensland, good-bye, White Australia; Good-bye Christians”: Australia’s South Sea Islander Community and Deportation, 1901–1908. The New Federalist, 4, 22–29.","u":"https://www.multiculturalaustralia.edu.au/doc/moore_ssi_deport.pdf","short":"Moore (2000)"},"moore2015":{"t":"Moore, C. (2015). The Pacific Islanders’ Fund and the Misappropriation of the Wages of Deceased Pacific Islanders by the Queensland Government. Australian Journal of Politics and History, 61(1), 1–18.","u":"https://www.assipj.com.au/southsea/wp-content/uploads/misappropriation-of-wages.pdf","short":"Moore (2015)"},"naaSSI":{"t":"National Archives of Australia. Fact sheet: South Sea Islanders.","u":"https://www.naa.gov.au/help-your-research/fact-sheets/south-sea-islanders","short":"NAA fact sheet"},"naaPILA":{"t":"National Archives of Australia. Pacific Island Labourers Act 1901 (learning resource).","u":"https://www.naa.gov.au/students-and-teachers/learning-resources/learning-resource-themes/society-and-culture/migration-and-multiculturalism/pacific-island-labourers-act-1901","short":"NAA, Pacific Island Labourers Act 1901"},"nma":{"t":"National Museum of Australia. “Federation Drought”. Defining Moments (website), updated 10 June 2025.","u":"https://www.nma.gov.au/defining-moments/resources/federation-drought","short":"National Museum of Australia, Federation Drought","note":"Sheep and cattle numbers, stock losses on the droving routes, foreclosures by banks, and the break-up of large stations. Many of its figures are for Australia as a whole. Its Queensland figure is for cattle only."},"peo":{"t":"Parliamentary Education Office. Pacific Islanders Labourers Act 1901 (with a General Certificate of Exemption dated 4 May 1907).","u":"http://peo.gov.au/understand-our-parliament/history-of-parliament/history-milestones/australian-parliament-history-timeline/events/pacific-islanders-labourers-act-1901","short":"Parliamentary Education Office"},"photoBundaberg":{"t":"Photograph: South Sea Islanders on the deck of a ship arriving at Bundaberg, 1895 (R. J. Cottell, from Pugh’s Almanac 1895, p. 149). State Library of Queensland negative 16954, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain).","u":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_50564_South_Sea_Islanders_on_the_deck_of_a_ship_arriving_at_Bundaberg,_1895.jpg","short":"Photograph, Bundaberg 1895"},"photoHambledon":{"t":"Photograph: South Sea Islander workers hoeing weeds on a sugar cane plantation at Hambledon, Queensland, about 1891. State Library of Queensland (photographer unidentified; no known copyright restrictions), via Wikimedia Commons.","u":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australian_South_Sea_Islander_workers_hoeing_weeds_on_a_sugar_cane_plantation_in_Hambledon,_Queensland,_ca._1891_(9205456322).jpg","short":"Photograph, Hambledon about 1891"},"pioneerMill":{"t":"Mackay history research. Pioneer Sugar Mill, 1867–1882 (John Spiller planted the first cane in the district on 1 June 1865).","u":"https://mackayhistory.org/research/sugar_mills/pioneer.html","short":"Pioneer Plantation, Mackay history"},"quassic1":{"t":"QUASSIC (Queensland United Australian South Sea Islander Council). Historical Information Sheet No 1: Defining Slavery, Indenture, and Blackbirding.","u":"https://www.quassic.org.au/no-1-defining-slavery-indenture-and-blackbirding","short":"QUASSIC sheet 1"},"quassic6":{"t":"QUASSIC. Historical Information Sheet No 6: Mortality and Pacific Islander Migrants in Colonial Queensland.","u":"https://www.quassic.org.au/no-6-mortality-and-pacific-islander-migrants-in-colonial-queensland","short":"QUASSIC sheet 6"},"quassic7":{"t":"QUASSIC. Historical Information Sheet No 7: Incidents of Abuse of Pacific Islanders during the Recruiting Process to Queensland.","u":"https://www.quassic.org.au/no-7-incidents-of-abuse-of-pacific-islanders-during-the-recruiting-process-to-queensland","short":"QUASSIC sheet 7"},"sarina":{"t":"Queensland Government. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community histories: Sarina (covers the Mackay district).","u":"https://www.qld.gov.au/firstnations/cultural-awareness-heritage-arts/community-histories/community-histories-s-t/community-histories-sarina","short":"Queensland Government, Sarina community history"},"yarrabah":{"t":"Queensland Government. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community histories: Yarrabah (covers the Cairns district).","u":"https://www.qld.gov.au/firstnations/cultural-awareness-heritage-arts/community-histories/community-histories-u-y/community-histories-yarrabah","short":"Queensland Government, Yarrabah community history"},"wall":{"t":"Queensland Heritage Register, entry 602230: South Sea Islander Wall, Mon Repos, Bundaberg Region (listed 1 October 2001).","u":"https://apps.des.qld.gov.au/heritage-register/detail/?id=602230","short":"Queensland Heritage Register 602230"},"qsa":{"t":"Queensland State Archives. Agency A2377, Native Police.","u":"https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/agencies/A2377","short":"Queensland State Archives, agency A2377"},"senate":{"t":"Parliament of Australia, Senate Legal and Constitutional References Committee (2006). Unfinished business: Indigenous stolen wages, Chapter 2: Life under the protection Acts.","u":"https://www.aph.gov.au/parliamentary_business/committees/senate/legal_and_constitutional_affairs/completed_inquiries/2004-07/stolen_wages/report/c02","short":"Senate stolen wages report, ch. 2"},"slq":{"t":"State Library of Queensland, John Oxley Library blog (2013). A White Australia (a review of the 1901 booklet “A White Australia: the Kanaka Labor Question”).","u":"https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/blog/white-australia","short":"State Library of Queensland blog"},"trove1885":{"t":"The Labour Trade. Newspaper report of 6 May 1885 on the voyage of the Hopeful, via Trove (National Library of Australia), article 174162604.","u":"http://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/rendition/nla.news-article174162604.txt","short":"Trove, The Labour Trade, 6 May 1885"},"uq":{"t":"University of Queensland. Unskilled labour migration into Queensland (Research Week 2010 paper on the Polynesian Labourers Act 1868 (Qld)).","u":"https://law.uq.edu.au/files/4284/Slavery-or-Salvation-Research-Week-2010.pdf","short":"University of Queensland paper"},"huntKennedy2006":{"t":"Douglas Hunt and K. H. Kennedy. “Bye Bye Blackbirder: the death of Ross Lewin”. Journal of the Royal Historical Society of Queensland, vol. 19, no. 5, 2006, pp. 805–823.","u":"https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/4138/","short":"Hunt and Kennedy, death of Ross Lewin (2006)","note":"Only the abstract could be read. The full paper is restricted."},"loganVeresdale":{"t":"Logan City Council. “Veresdale”. Suburb history page.","u":"https://www.logan.qld.gov.au/about-council/about-the-city-of-logan/suburbs/veresdale","short":"Logan City Council, Veresdale","note":"The Townsvale plantation, the Don Juan in 1863 and Ross Lewin."},"slqHayes":{"t":"Simon Miller. “Bully Hayes – Pacific pirate: truth or fiction?”. State Library of Queensland, John Oxley Library blog, 12 September 2013.","u":"https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/blog/bully-hayes-pacific-pirate-truth-or-fiction","short":"SLQ blog, Bully Hayes (2013)","note":"The post includes unproven allegations about Hayes that are not repeated here."},"spicerBecke":{"t":"Chrystopher J. Spicer. “The Granite and the Rainbow: Towards a New Biography of Louis Becke”. Journal of the Association for the Study of Australian Literature.","u":"https://openjournals.library.sydney.edu.au/JASAL/article/download/14907/13571","short":"Spicer, a new biography of Louis Becke","note":"How Becke wrote about Hayes. The paper quotes an offensive word from Becke’s stories only by name. It is not repeated here."}},"folklore":{"intro":"Stories about the labour trade are often told as adventures. Real people were taken from their islands, and some were deceived or forced. This page tells only two stories. Both are about men whose reputations grew in the telling, and both can be found in published sources. They are about recruiters and ship captains. They are not about Pacific Islander people. Each is labelled and has a “What we actually know” part.","banner":{"head":"Stories people told, not proven history","text":["Read this first: the stories on this page are folklore. They are not proven history.","These are yarns, legends and tall tales that people have told about this period. Each one really exists in a published source, and the source is linked. But the stories themselves are largely unproven. Some may be partly true. Some are probably wrong.","Each story has a “What we actually know” part. It says what is documented and why the rest is doubtful. Do not use anything on this page as a fact in an essay or a test.","This page does not include Aboriginal Dreaming stories or any sacred or restricted stories."]},"stories":[{"title":"Ross Lewin, “the greatest man-stealer of them all”","where":"South Pacific and the Logan River · 1863 to 1874","text":["Ross Lewin has a reputation as the worst of the men who recruited Pacific Islanders for work in Queensland. Missionaries called him a slave trader. In one telling, Royal Navy captains chased him and never caught him. They called him the greatest man-stealer of them all.","He was killed on Tanna, in what is now Vanuatu, in April 1874. Many people at the time felt he had got what he deserved."],"from":"The 2006 paper “Bye Bye Blackbirder: the death of Ross Lewin”, by Douglas Hunt and K. H. Kennedy in the Journal of the Royal Historical Society of Queensland, asks how much of this reputation can be proved. Its abstract is online. Logan City Council’s history of Veresdale also describes Lewin.","known":["What is documented: Lewin was the recruiting agent on the schooner Don Juan, which brought the first Pacific Islander labourers to Robert Towns’s plantation on the Logan River in August 1863. He kept recruiting into the early 1870s. Logan City Council says he became notorious for illegal recruiting, known as blackbirding. He was killed on Tanna in April 1874.","Why the rest is doubtful: The paper’s abstract says Lewin’s reputation was “variously embellished, accepted, and perpetuated” in popular accounts and in academic histories. The authors say they found “a mix of conflicting evidence and assertion” and could not give a clear answer to the question “Who was Ross Lewin?” They say the Islander oral tradition of blackbirding gives a different view from the written record. Only the abstract of the paper could be read for this resource. This resource does not say how many people Lewin recruited or by what methods, because the sources read do not say."],"src":["huntKennedy2006","loganVeresdale"]},{"title":"Bully Hayes, the “pirate of the Pacific”","where":"Pacific Islands, and Louis Becke’s stories · 1874 to 1913","text":["William Henry “Bully” Hayes was an American ship captain in the Pacific. In story books he is the last of the Pacific pirates. The writer Louis Becke, who spent time in Queensland, wrote many stories about him. Becke said he sailed with Hayes for about two years, and in some tellings for nearly four years.","Some stories say that when Hayes died in 1877 he was shot and drowned, or even eaten by sharks."],"from":"The State Library of Queensland published a 2013 blog post, “Bully Hayes – Pacific pirate: truth or fiction?”. A paper by Chrystopher Spicer in the Journal of the Association for the Study of Australian Literature, “The Granite and the Rainbow: Towards a New Biography of Louis Becke”, also discusses it.","known":["What is documented: Hayes was a ship captain and trader known as a swindler. He was involved in blackbirding in the Pacific. The State Library says it does not appear that he shipped labourers to Queensland. Becke sailed on Hayes’s ship Leonora in 1874, and the ship was wrecked at Kosrae in March 1874. Hayes was killed in March 1877, aged 47. The mate’s report, published in the San Francisco Post, says the cook hit him on the head with a boom crutch, and that no firearms were found on him.","Why the stories are doubtful: The State Library says Becke sailed with Hayes for a little under two months. Spicer says about ten weeks, and that there is documentary evidence for a few weeks together aboard the Leonora. Becke’s claim of two years or more is unsupported. Becke also said he was arrested for piracy by the captain of HMS Rosario, but Spicer says there is no evidence of that in the captain’s report. The library calls Becke “a very unreliable witness” and says Hayes’s reputation as a pirate is “largely based on unreliable and largely fictitious accounts”. It says the shark story is a later embroidery on the mate’s report."],"src":["slqHayes","spicerBecke"]}]},"folkTeach":[{"q":"Why might one man become the main example of the whole labour trade in later stories? What is lost when the trade is told through its worst recruiter?","a":["The abstract says Lewin’s name “epitomises” the brutal reputation of the trade. One villain is easier to remember than a whole system of recruiters, ship owners, planters and officials.","Ask students whose evidence is missing from stories about Lewin. The authors point to the Islander oral tradition of blackbirding. Discuss why oral tradition and written records may give different pictures."]},{"q":"Louis Becke wrote about Hayes as if he had been there for years. How should a reader treat a book that mixes real events with invented ones?","a":["Spicer describes Becke’s writing as “faction”: fiction based on or written around facts. A reader should check each claim against documents made at the time, such as the report of the captain of HMS Rosario.","Ask students to list what each source says about how long Becke sailed with Hayes: Becke says about two years or more, the State Library says a little under two months, and Spicer says about ten weeks. Ask why documents should be trusted over Becke’s own claim."]}]};
